window.__SLIDES&&window.__SLIDES('c2',{"s":["<section id=\"c2-tldr\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:28px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Allocation · 2020–2026</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Learning partner capability; one line starts at intent</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px\">\n<div style=\"width:880px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:28px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Background · before the series</p><ul style=\"font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\"><li><b>Fixed-score allocation</b> · Multi-robot allocation assigned robots to tasks from fixed skill or cost scores (Notomista et al. 2019).</li><li><b>Fixed-time scheduling</b> · Human-robot schedulers took task times as fixed or set-bounded; fair allocation (Claure 2019) ignored deadlines.</li><li><b>Intent inference</b> · Goal inference and inverse optimal control (Mainprice et al.) predict motion, not what a person can do.</li><li><b>Ad hoc teamwork</b> · AHT (Stone et al. 2010; Mirsky et al. 2022) covers unknown partners; its models were task-specific.</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Problem</p><p style=\"font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">Work should go to whoever can do it, but capabilities are unknown or change, so each paper must learn or hedge them.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Idea</p><p style=\"font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">Model capability as a score, curve, distribution or vector, estimated from progress, times or simulated trajectories, then plan around it.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<img src=\"assets/img/c2_tldr.webp\" alt=\"Lineage of the key papers in this work tree, drawn by year with arrows from parent to child\" style=\"width:736px;height:260px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3;color:#5B6472\">Solid: Approx. Bayes to CAMO to CE-CM; the rest join by topic White pills are guests: papers whose home is another tree, included because they sit on a lineage here.</p>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px;background:#EFEBE0;border-radius:16px;padding:18px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Terms</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\"><b style=\"color:#78801E\">Capability</b> · what an agent can do, or how well</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\"><b style=\"color:#78801E\">Task allocation</b> · deciding which agent does which task</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\"><b style=\"color:#78801E\">AHT</b> · teaming with unknown partners, no pre-coordination</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\"><b style=\"color:#78801E\">ABC</b> · keep guesses whose simulation matches the data</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Capability-aware allocation</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">6 papers (1 guests) · 2020–2026 · Approx. Bayes → CAMO → CE-CM</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"c2-assume\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:28px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Allocation · Assumptions</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">The line assumes a hidden quantity a model can test</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:12px\"><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start;padding-bottom:10px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"width:500px;font-size:28px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;color:#78801E\">A hidden quantity</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">Capability as score, curve, distribution, limit or binary vector; Approx. Bayes infers a reach intent (a 3D target) instead.</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start;padding-bottom:10px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"width:500px;font-size:28px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;color:#78801E\">Tasks and goals are given</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">Task list, cost or reward are known; CE-CM also fixes goal and reward. Approx. Bayes infers the goal itself.</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start;padding-bottom:10px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"width:500px;font-size:28px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;color:#78801E\">A model to compare against</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">Nominal dynamics, a prior, a simulator or a joint planner gives predictions; Approx. Bayes, CAMO, CE-CM simulate.</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start;padding-bottom:10px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"width:500px;font-size:28px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;color:#78801E\">Hidden quantity mostly stable</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">Adaptive alloc. tracks drift, Task prof. learning; Robust sched., CAMO, CE-CM treat it as fixed.</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start;padding-bottom:10px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"width:500px;font-size:28px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;color:#78801E\">Control differs</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">Three allocators command every agent; CAMO only watches; Approx. Bayes and CE-CM control only themselves.</p></div></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:20px;align-items:center\"><div style=\"width:760px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F5F7E3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Builds on</p><p style=\"font-size:26px;line-height:1.3\">Fixed-score robot allocation (Notomista 2019), chance-constrained scheduling, and ABC (Rubin 1984) via Approx. Bayes.</p></div><svg width=\"48\" height=\"30\" viewBox=\"0 0 48 30\" style=\"flex:none;display:block;\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M0,9.0 L28.799999999999997,9.0 L28.799999999999997,0 L48.0,15.0 L28.799999999999997,30.0 L28.799999999999997,21.0 L0,21.0 Z\" fill=\"#78801E\"/></svg><div style=\"width:816px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F5F7E3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">So the series achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:26px;line-height:1.3\">From 6-robot demos (Adaptive alloc.) and a 16-cube cobot task (Approx. Bayes) to capability estimates on 15-person data (CE-CM).</p></div></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Capability-aware allocation</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">6 papers (1 guests) · 2020–2026 · Approx. Bayes → CAMO → CE-CM</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"c2-method\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Allocation · Evolution</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">From score updates to simulate-and-compare inference</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"background:#F5F7E3;border-radius:16px;padding:18px 32px\"><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.35\">Allocators see progress or times directly; a partner&#x27;s hidden traits show only in motion, so they are inferred by simulating guesses, as Approx. Bayes did for intent.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:8px\">\n<img src=\"assets/img/c2_method.webp\" alt=\"Lineage of every paper in the work tree by year, arrows from parent to child\" style=\"width:1664px;height:470px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3;color:#5B6472\">Each arrow points from a paper to the paper that builds on it. Dark pills mark the key papers. White pills are guests: papers whose home is another tree, included because they sit on a lineage here. Papers without an arrow are tied to the series only by baseline comparisons.</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Capability-aware allocation</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">6 papers (1 guests) · 2020–2026 · Approx. Bayes → CAMO → CE-CM</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"c2-stages\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:28px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Allocation · Stages</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">The series unfolds in three stages</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:16px;align-items:stretch\"><div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:18px;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-top:8px solid #C8651B;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 32px\"><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:32px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.15;color:#C8651B\">1 · Update from feedback</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3;color:#5B6472\">2020–2022</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:10px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#F5F7E3;border:2px solid #78801E;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">Adaptive alloc.</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#F5F7E3;border:2px solid #78801E;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">Task prof.</p></div><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.35\">Adaptive alloc. shifts a skill score by the progress gap; Task prof. fits a Kalman-updated learning curve per person.</p></div><svg width=\"40\" height=\"28\" viewBox=\"0 0 40 28\" style=\"flex:none;display:block;\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M0,8.4 L24.0,8.4 L24.0,0 L40.0,14.0 L24.0,28.0 L24.0,19.599999999999998 L0,19.599999999999998 Z\" fill=\"#78801E\"/></svg><div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:18px;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-top:8px solid #3E6FD9;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 32px\"><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:32px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.15;color:#3E6FD9\">2 · Hedge against risk</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3;color:#5B6472\">2023</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:10px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#F5F7E3;border:2px solid #78801E;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">Robust sched.</p></div><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.35\">Takes capability distributions as given and minimises the CVaR of missing a requirement in one pre-mission plan.</p></div><svg width=\"40\" height=\"28\" viewBox=\"0 0 40 28\" style=\"flex:none;display:block;\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M0,8.4 L24.0,8.4 L24.0,0 L40.0,14.0 L24.0,28.0 L24.0,19.599999999999998 L0,19.599999999999998 Z\" fill=\"#78801E\"/></svg><div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:18px;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-top:8px solid #8E4FC9;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 32px\"><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:32px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.15;color:#8E4FC9\">3 · Infer by simulating</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3;color:#5B6472\">2022–2026</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:10px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#F5F7E3;border:2px solid #78801E;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">Approx. Bayes</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#F5F7E3;border:2px solid #78801E;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">CAMO</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#F5F7E3;border:2px solid #78801E;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">CE-CM</p></div><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.35\">Sample guesses, simulate, keep matches (ABC): Approx. Bayes for intent, CAMO for continuous limits, CE-CM for binary vectors, online.</p></div></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Capability-aware allocation</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">6 papers (1 guests) · 2020–2026 · Approx. Bayes → CAMO → CE-CM</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"c2-s01\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Allocation · Adaptive alloc. 2020 · 1 of 6</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Robots re-score themselves from observed progress</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Builds on Notomista et al. (ECC 2019, outside the tree): allocation and execution solved together with fixed skill scores. CE-CM cites it as related work only.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Task progress is measurable; nominal model</li><li>Target share of robots per task is given</li><li>All robots follow the allocation</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Update law: change a robot&#x27;s skill score by the gap between expected and actual progress, then re-solve the allocation.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/c2_s01.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:62px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#F5F7E3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">No later member inherits it; CE-CM cites it as centralised parameter estimation.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F5F7E3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Robotarium, 6 robots and 6 tasks: all 6 targets reached in about 1 min despite hidden no-go zones; no baselines.</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Adaptive Task Allocation for Heterogeneous Multi-Robot Teams with Evolving and Unknown Robot Capabilities</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Emam et al. · ICRA 2020</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"c2-s02\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Allocation · Approx. Bayes 2022 · 2 of 6 · Guest</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Real-time intent inference by simulating reaches</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Home: Human models and mutual adaptation tree; here it is the source of CAMO&#x27;s ABC loop and reach controller. Builds on ABC (Rubin 1984) and Zanchettin &amp; Rocco.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Known reach simulator (9-DoF human arm)</li><li>Intent is one 3D target; mocap and gaze tracked</li><li>Hand-designed priors; turn-based pick-and-place</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Intent as ABC: sample targets, simulate reaches, keep matches. A neural surrogate with grid caching makes it real-time (9,100 samples in 0.01 s).</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/c2_s02.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:62px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#F5F7E3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">CAMO reuses its reach controller and rejection-sampling idea to infer joint limits, not intent; CE-CM inherits via CAMO.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F5F7E3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">UR5e + human, 16 cubes: mean task time 36.49 s vs 40.69 s for a retreat-triggered baseline (10 runs per method, no significance test).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Intuitive &amp; Efficient Human-robot Collaboration via Real-time Approximate Bayesian Inference</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Felip et al. · IROS 2022</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"c2-s03\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Allocation · Task prof. 2022 · 3 of 6</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">A scheduler that learns how fast each person learns</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Builds on chance-constrained scheduling, earliest-deadline-first, Kalman filtering and learning-curve work (all outside the tree). CE-CM only cites it.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Exponential learning curve, normal times</li><li>Population prior; robot sees every duration</li><li>Fixed robot times; fatigue ignored</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Kalman-updated learning curve per person, a fast Gaussian deadline-risk bound, and search with a bonus for trying new person-task pairs.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/c2_s03.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:62px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#F5F7E3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">No later member inherits it; CE-CM cites it as centralised proficiency estimation.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F5F7E3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Simulation, 20 tasks: +44.8% makespan gain over a fixed schedule; LEGO user study with n = 90.</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Coordinating Human-Robot Teams with Dynamic and Stochastic Task Proficiencies</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Liu et al. · THRI 2022</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"c2-s04\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Allocation · Robust sched. 2023 · 4 of 6</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Hedge allocation against the worst-case shortfall</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Builds on the authors&#x27; deterministic model [17], STRATA&#x27;s capability vectors and CVaR (all outside the tree). Compares against STRATA; CE-CM cites it as related work.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Capability distributions known (Gaussian in tests)</li><li>One central plan before the mission</li><li>Fixed tasks, places and durations</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">One mixed-integer program minimising energy, time and the CVaR of missing a requirement, then a split of species flows into agent routes.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/c2_s04.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:62px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#F5F7E3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">No later member uses its CVaR; CE-CM cites it as centralised capability estimation.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F5F7E3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Capture the flag: 293 vs 203 wins per 500 games against STRATA; pandemic case plans up to 140 agents, 40 tasks.</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Robust Task Scheduling for Heterogeneous Robot Teams under Capability Uncertainty</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Fu et al. · T-RO 2023</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"c2-s05\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Allocation · CAMO 2024 · 5 of 6</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Infer a person&#x27;s joint limits just by watching</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Reuses Approx. Bayes&#x27;s reach controller and ABC rejection loop; also KDE and DTW (outside). Closest prior work, Bestick et al., needs active queries.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Exact simulator of the person; goal known</li><li>Fixed joint limits; simulated people only</li><li>Only reachable attempts are observed</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Targets capability, not goal: sample limit guesses, simulate, keep those near the observed path (DTW), fit a density per task, multiply.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/c2_s05.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:62px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#F5F7E3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">CE-CM keeps its sample-simulate-compare loop for discrete capabilities, online.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F5F7E3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Simulated 7-DOF arm: ~25 cm error on unseen goals vs ~160 cm from observed limits (Fig. 3, read by eye).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Probabilistic Inference of Human Capabilities from Passive Observations</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Tisnikar et al. · IROS 2024</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"c2-s06\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Allocation · CE-CM 2026 · 6 of 6</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Learn what a partner can&#x27;t do, then plan around it</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Builds on CAMO&#x27;s sample-simulate-compare inference, itself reused from Approx. Bayes, and contextual MDPs. Cites the three allocators as related work.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Hidden fixed binary capabilities; goal known</li><li>Two agents; ego controls only itself</li><li>Partner plans, and corrects the robot</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Discrete task-invariant capability vectors inferred online across tasks, plus CE-CM-Div, which tests guesses against diverse rollouts.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/c2_s06.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:200px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F5F7E3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Human study (15 people, 225 trajectories): Hamming error 0.27-0.39 with CE-CM-Div vs 0.46-0.75 with CE-CM.</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Partner Capability Estimation for Task-Agnostic Adaptation in Ad-Hoc Teamwork</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Tisnikar et al. · arXiv 2026</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"c2-eval\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Allocation · Evaluation</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Six papers, six benchmarks, no shared yardstick</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:560px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:20px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:10px;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-radius:16px;padding:22px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Benchmarks and baselines</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">Adaptive alloc., Robust sched. use simulation or demos; Task prof. adds a LEGO study; Approx. Bayes a real UR5e.</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">CAMO uses a simulated arm and person; CE-CM adds TidyUP, Overcooked and a 15-person human study.</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">Only Approx. Bayes, Robust sched. and CE-CM compare against published or ablated baselines.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px;background:#F5F7E3;border-radius:16px;padding:18px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Reading</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.35\">Metrics differ (completion, makespan, wins, cm, Hamming error): read each result only inside its own paper.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-radius:16px;padding:20px 32px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472;padding-bottom:4px\">Headline result of each paper</p><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#78801E\">Adaptive alloc.</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">Robotarium, 6 robots, 6 tasks</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">All 6 targets in ~1 min; no baselines</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#78801E\">Approx. Bayes</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">UR5e + human, 16-cube packing</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">Task time 36.49 s vs 40.69 s baseline</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#78801E\">Task prof.</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">Simulation, 20 tasks; LEGO n = 90</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">+44.8% makespan gain vs fixed schedule</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#78801E\">Robust sched.</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">Capture the flag, 500 games</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">293 vs 203 wins against STRATA</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#78801E\">CAMO</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">Simulated 7-DOF arm, unseen goals</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">~25 cm vs ~160 cm joint-space error</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#78801E\">CE-CM</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">Overcooked human study, 15 people</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">Hamming error 0.27-0.39 vs 0.46-0.75</p></div></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Capability-aware allocation</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">6 papers (1 guests) · 2020–2026 · Approx. Bayes → CAMO → CE-CM</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"c2-open\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:28px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#78801E\">Allocation · Open questions</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">What the series still leaves open</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\"><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:center;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-left:8px solid #78801E;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 36px\"><p style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:64px;font-weight:600;line-height:1;color:#78801E;width:56px\">1</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:32px;line-height:1.35\">The allocators control every agent; none handles a partner it cannot command, which AHT requires.</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:center;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-left:8px solid #78801E;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 36px\"><p style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:64px;font-weight:600;line-height:1;color:#78801E;width:56px\">2</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:32px;line-height:1.35\">CAMO and CE-CM assume fixed capabilities and a known goal; CE-CM covers dyads and offline human data only.</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:center;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-left:8px solid #78801E;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 36px\"><p style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:64px;font-weight:600;line-height:1;color:#78801E;width:56px\">3</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:32px;line-height:1.35\">Approx. Bayes gives no participant count or test; most evidence is simulation or small demos.</p></div></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Capability-aware allocation</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">6 papers (1 guests) · 2020–2026 · Approx. Bayes → CAMO → CE-CM</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>"],"n":["Members (chronological): Adaptive alloc. 2020 (Emam et al., ICRA), Approx. Bayes 2022 (Felip et al., IROS; GUEST, home tree &#x27;Human models and mutual adaptation&#x27;), Task prof. 2022 (Liu et al., THRI), Robust sched. 2023 (Fu et al., T-RO), CAMO 2024 (Tisnikar et al., IROS), CE-CM 2026 (Tisnikar et al., arXiv). Relation to ad hoc teamwork: the AHT survey (Mirsky et al. 2022) and the agent-modelling survey (Albrecht and Stone 2018) treat multi-robot task allocation as a related problem, not AHT. Adaptive alloc., Task prof. and Robust sched. sit in that neighbouring field: a central allocator or scheduler commands every agent, so there is no uncontrolled partner and nothing is &#x27;ad hoc&#x27;. Their tags place them outside both surveys (Robust sched. loosely ST1, Adaptive alloc. loosely ST2/ST4, Task prof. ST2/ST3 for learning human teammates). CAMO estimates a human&#x27;s capabilities by passive observation (Mirsky ST2) without teaming. Only CE-CM is AHT proper (ST2, heterogeneous teammates; type-based reasoning). Edges: two inheritance edges. Approx. Bayes -&gt; CAMO (kind 1, reuses a component: the differential-kinematics reach controller and the ABC rejection-sampling idea; CAMO&#x27;s text cites it for both) and CAMO -&gt; CE-CM (kind 0; the CE-CM paper states it reuses CAMO&#x27;s simulate-and-compare inference). Three comparison/background edges, Adaptive alloc., Task prof., Robust sched. -&gt; CE-CM (kind 2): CE-CM&#x27;s related work lists them as centralised estimation of proficiency or capability parameters that cannot serve uncontrollable partners. The three allocation papers have no edge to each other or to CAMO in the matched reference lists, so the lineage figure shows a two-edge solid chain, Approx. Bayes -&gt; CAMO -&gt; CE-CM, and three allocation members joined by topic. The guest changes the story: the capability-inference line reaches back to intent inference in human-robot collaboration (Approx. Bayes infers what the hand is reaching for; CAMO and CE-CM infer what the person can do). The tree is a topic cluster plus one real lineage. Background cited in slides: Notomista 2019 (Adaptive alloc.), Claure 2019 (Task prof.), STRATA and CVaR (Robust sched.), Mainprice et al. and Bestick et al. (CAMO), Stone et al. 2010, Albrecht and Stone, Czechowski and Oliehoek (CE-CM). Approx. Bayes is not capability or allocation work and has no edge to the three allocators; it belongs only through the CAMO edge. Idea shared: treat capability as a quantity to be estimated or hedged, then allocate or plan around it.","Approx. Bayes (guest) assumes a known 9-DoF reach simulator, intent = one 3D target, mocap and gaze sensing, hand-designed priors and a turn-based task; its hidden quantity is intent, not capability, and the robot acts on it (controls only itself). Shared assumptions: capability is a hidden quantity (score, learning curve, distribution, joint limit, binary vector); tasks, goals and costs are given; a model or simulator supplies predictions; capability is fixed except in Adaptive alloc. (drift, with slow recovery) and Task prof. (exponential learning); control is central in the first three, passive in CAMO, ego-only in CE-CM. Where assumptions are relaxed: Adaptive alloc. allows unknown, changing capability but needs a nominal model and a given target split. Task prof. allows learning humans and stochastic times but assumes exponential learning and normal noise. Robust sched. allows uncertainty but only as known distributions in a fixed pre-mission plan. CAMO needs an exact simulator and known goals. CE-CM drops the single-trajectory assumption via CE-CM-Div, drops population pre-training, adds online use, but keeps binary fixed capabilities, known goal and reward, two agents, and a partner who can override the robot. CE-CM also assumes a partner who corrects the ego agent when their actions conflict (the human study lets participants override the assistant). Hidden capability vectors are 20-dimensional in TidyUP and 10-dimensional in Overcooked. Sources: notes and slides of p29, p79, p32, p86, p28; related-work paragraph of CE-CM (txt of idx 21).","Three stages. (1) Update from feedback: Adaptive alloc. s_ij += beta_1 * alpha_ij * DeltaV with DeltaV = expected minus actual cost; Task prof. Kalman filter over (c, k, beta) of an exponential curve, Gaussian deadline-risk bound, evolutionary search with exploration weight lambda. (2) Hedge: Robust sched. minimises energy, time and CVaR of the shortfall in one mixed-integer program (sample-average approximation, L-shaped algorithm), then rounds and splits species flows. (3) Infer by simulating: Approx. Bayes (guest; sample a target from three priors, simulate a reach, keep if windowed MSE &lt; epsilon; neural surrogate, 9,100 grid samples in 0.01 s), CAMO (sample b limit guesses, simulate, keep within epsilon by DTW, KDE per task, multiply over tasks) and CE-CM (sample 500 binary capability guesses, joint-plan under each, accept if close to the observation, Bernoulli posterior with threshold psi, plan next task; CE-CM-Div accepts if any of up to 50 diverse rollouts matches). Inheritance edges: Approx. Bayes -&gt; CAMO (kind 1) and CAMO -&gt; CE-CM (kind 0). Stage 1 and 2 papers are placed by topic. Why the shift: the allocators measure progress or times directly after assigning a task; with a free partner, only trajectories are seen and a hypothesis can be tested only through the joint behaviour it induces, so CE-CM simulates. Source: CE-CM Section 4 and Algorithm 1; CAMO Section IV; p29 Sections III-IV; p79 Sections 4-8; p32 Sections III-IV.","Three stages. (1) Update from feedback: Adaptive alloc. s_ij += beta_1 * alpha_ij * DeltaV with DeltaV = expected minus actual cost; Task prof. Kalman filter over (c, k, beta) of an exponential curve, Gaussian deadline-risk bound, evolutionary search with exploration weight lambda. (2) Hedge: Robust sched. minimises energy, time and CVaR of the shortfall in one mixed-integer program (sample-average approximation, L-shaped algorithm), then rounds and splits species flows. (3) Infer by simulating: Approx. Bayes (guest; sample a target from three priors, simulate a reach, keep if windowed MSE &lt; epsilon; neural surrogate, 9,100 grid samples in 0.01 s), CAMO (sample b limit guesses, simulate, keep within epsilon by DTW, KDE per task, multiply over tasks) and CE-CM (sample 500 binary capability guesses, joint-plan under each, accept if close to the observation, Bernoulli posterior with threshold psi, plan next task; CE-CM-Div accepts if any of up to 50 diverse rollouts matches). Inheritance edges: Approx. Bayes -&gt; CAMO (kind 1) and CAMO -&gt; CE-CM (kind 0). Stage 1 and 2 papers are placed by topic. Why the shift: the allocators measure progress or times directly after assigning a task; with a free partner, only trajectories are seen and a hypothesis can be tested only through the joint behaviour it induces, so CE-CM simulates. Source: CE-CM Section 4 and Algorithm 1; CAMO Section IV; p29 Sections III-IV; p79 Sections 4-8; p32 Sections III-IV.","Emam, Mayya, Notomista, Bohannon, Egerstedt, ICRA 2020 (p29). Venue ICRA 2020 comes from the file name; the PDF carries only an arXiv stamp (6 Mar 2020). Edges: it has no parent inside the tree. Outside parent: Notomista et al., ECC 2019 [8] (allocation plus execution with fixed specialization). Child side: CE-CM cites it (kind 2, related work) in the paragraph on estimating partner parameters (task proficiency [46,47,48]) in centralised allocation and scheduling; CE-CM says these violate the assumption that partners are uncontrollable. It is not AHT: every robot is controlled by the allocation. Method: skill score s_ij &gt;= 0 (larger = robot i better suited to task j). Update s_ij[k+1] = s_ij[k] + beta_1 * alpha_ij * DeltaV_ij, DeltaV = expected cost under nominal dynamics minus actual cost; only the assigned task&#x27;s score changes. Special case adds a slow recovery term toward the nominal score (beta_2 much smaller than beta_1). The allocation is a mixed-integer quadratic program with control-barrier-function constraints. Numbers (Section V, text and Figs. 5-6, no results table): experiment A, 3 quadcopters (virtual) + 3 ground robots, 6 go-to-goal tasks, all s_ij start at 1, all 6 targets reached in about 1 minute after several re-configurations. Experiment B, 2+2 robots, 4 tasks, no-go states switched halfway: 4 of 4 tasks before the switch, 3 of 4 robots after; the authors blame beta_2. Caveats: no baselines, no repeated-run statistics, no timing or scaling; this is a feasibility demonstration. Communication model and robot-count limits are not stated.","Felip, Gonzalez-Aguirre, Nachman (Intel Labs), IROS 2022 (p77). The PDF is the arXiv version (2205.08657v1, May 2022) and prints no venue, so IROS 2022 comes from the file name. GUEST: home tree f1 &#x27;Human models and mutual adaptation&#x27;; it is in this tree only because of the CAMO edge. Edge used: Approx. Bayes -&gt; CAMO is kind 1 (reuses a component). CAMO&#x27;s text says rejection-sampling ABC &#x27;has found its use in human-robot interaction scenarios [27], [28]&#x27; ([28] = this paper), then says those implementations infer parameters that explain the goal, whereas CAMO estimates a property of the human collaborator; and it uses &#x27;a differential kinematics controller as described in [28]&#x27;, parameterised with joint limits, to simulate reaches. So the borrowed parts are the simulator-plus-rejection-sampling loop and the reach controller; the target changes from intent to capability. CE-CM does not build on this paper directly in the text we matched; its link is through CAMO (CE-CM -&gt; CAMO kind 0). Approx. Bayes has no edge to Adaptive alloc., Task prof. or Robust sched. Method (Section III, Algorithm 1): draw a target z from a prior, simulate a reach x_hat = g(z), keep z if the windowed mean squared error to the observed partial path is below epsilon. Priors: Normal near the human, Normal on the gaze-table intersection, Gaussian mixture with a mode per detected object. Simulator about 150 ms per 3 s trajectory; a 3-layer surrogate (trained on 10,000 simulated paths, mean error 1.7 cm) evaluates 9,100 samples in 0.01 s instead of 1365 s (&gt;100,000x). The robot starts its reach once enough certainty that its object is away from the human&#x27;s intent; how &#x27;enough&#x27; is thresholded is not given. Numbers (Table I, no units printed, seconds read from Fig. 6): task time solo robot 64.55, solo human 57.05, collaborative baseline 40.69 +-6.68, intent prediction 36.49 +-2.78; functional delay -0.85 to -2.91 s. 10 task executions per method, 16 pick-and-place per execution. Caveats: number of human participants not stated; no significance test; the +- is undefined; assumptions are our reading (no assumptions section); the paper says the surrogate might not generalise to other kinematics. Code not checked. Numbers are not comparable with the other members.","Liu, Natarajan, Gombolay, THRI 2022 (p79). The PDF is an ACM POMACS-format manuscript dated Aug 2020 and does not name THRI; the venue comes from the file name. Edges: no parent inside the tree. Outside: chance-constrained programming, EDF scheduling, adaptive Kalman filter (Akhlaghi et al. 2017), exponential learning curves, Gombolay et al. on robot-led scheduling. The paper names no single base method, so the &#x27;builds on&#x27; list is a synthesis. Child side: CE-CM cites it (kind 2) as proficiency estimation inside a centralised scheduler; the robot decides who does what, so it is not AHT in the survey sense, although the humans&#x27; skill is unknown and changes. Method: minimise makespan z1 + lambda * z2, z2 penalising repeated agent-task pairs (exploration), subject to deadlines holding with probability 1 - epsilon. Human task time y = c + k exp(-beta i) with normal noise. A line-searched Gaussian bound is &gt;10^3 times faster than the exact finish-time distribution (&lt;0.1 s vs 5.56 / 51.69 / 201.14 s for 25 / 50 / 75 tasks), with 8.44 +/- 10.1% extra conservatism at 95% robustness. Numbers: Tables 4-5, aggregate makespan gain over ten rounds versus keeping the first EDF schedule: 20 tasks 0.6 (evolutionary only), 40.4 (+ adaptive updates), 44.8 +/- 6.4 (full); 50 tasks -2.7, 15.0, 18.9 +/- 5.5. Learning-curve update cut prediction error 59.2 +/- 25.3%. User study: 96 recruited, 90 analysed, Sawyer robot, 5 rounds of 6 LEGO tasks; makespan gain differed by strategy (Kruskal-Wallis p = 0.00359) and working alliance (p = 0.0251); trust did not differ overall. Caveats: the paper reports inconsistent p-values for alliance and makespan across abstract, intro, discussion and results (Section 10.7 used). After matching, Fig. 13d shows p = 0.053 and 0.062 while the text calls them significant. LEGO assembly, short horizon, cross-sectional.","Fu, Smith, Rizzo, Castanier, Ghaffari, Barton, T-RO 2023 (p32; framework name CTAS). The PDF is arXiv v3 (Nov 2022) and does not print the journal, so the venue comes from the file name. Edges: no parent inside the tree. Outside: the authors&#x27; deterministic model [17], STRATA [53] (baseline; variance penalty on capability), CVaR from risk-aware robotics and finance. Child side: CE-CM cites it (kind 2) next to Adaptive alloc. and Task prof. in the paragraph on parameter estimation in centralised allocation. Our reading: Robust sched. takes capability distributions as known and does not learn them, so CE-CM&#x27;s grouping is loose. It is outside both AHT surveys; loosely Mirsky ST1 (capability representation). Method: species have random capability vectors; a task needs a binary and/or function of team capability. Objective C_e*energy + C_q*time + C_h*sum of task CVaR risks (CVaR = expected cost of the worst beta share of cases). Sample-average approximation plus an L-shaped algorithm keep it tractable; flow decomposition then rounds up and splits species flows into per-agent routes. Matching only expected capability succeeds about half the time per requirement (about 0.25 per task on average). Numbers: Fig. 9, ten sets of 500 games: CTAS vs STRATA 293 (+/-11.3) vs 203 (+/-11.6), 4 draws; CTAS vs random 324 vs 151. Blue/green assignment is inferred from row order. Mean assignment time STRATA 0.43 s, CTAS 0.04 s. Pandemic (Table XII, 16 tasks, 21 agents): +5.3% energy over CTAS-D (no risk term) raises success for 5 of 16 tasks, e.g. task 9 from 0.5 to 1.0. 32 mission cases, up to 140 agents and 40 tasks, 120 s limit. Caveats: simulation only; plan is fixed, with communication and recovery left to future work. Heat-map values in Figs. 11-15 not read.","Tisnikar, Canal, Leonetti, IROS 2024 (p86). CAMO = CApability Modeling from Observations. Edges: parent inside the tree is the guest Approx. Bayes (Felip et al., IROS 2022; home tree &#x27;Human models and mutual adaptation&#x27;): kind 1, CAMO reuses its differential-kinematics reach controller [28] and the ABC rejection-sampling idea, but infers capability (joint limits) rather than the reach goal, as its text says. None of the three allocation papers is cited in its text we could match. Outside: ABC (Beaumont), a related Felip et al. 2019 scene-understanding ABC paper [27], DTW. Child side: CE-CM states it &#x27;reuses CAMO&#x27;s inference principle&#x27; (kind 0). It is the bridge in this tree: it estimates a human partner&#x27;s limits like the allocation papers do, but passively and by simulation, which is what CE-CM adopts. It is not itself AHT: the robot watches and is not yet teaming. Method (Algorithm 1): draw b guesses from a prior; simulate a trajectory for the observed goal; keep guesses within epsilon (DTW); Gaussian KDE per task; multiply densities over tasks; estimate = highest summed log density. Keeping the whole set rather than one vector leaves room for later, incompatible tasks. 2000 uniform samples per task for the 7-DOF arm; 5000 Latin Hypercube samples (at most 500 kept) for the 9-DOF human model. Numbers: Fig. 3 bars about 163 cm (observed limits), 22 cm (CAMO-JS, joint trajectories), 28 cm (CAMO-CS, hand paths only); the text says only &#x27;around 25 cm&#x27;. Estimate converges within 3 tasks (Fig. 2 read by eye). Block stacking (4 blocks, about 75% of labels are &#x27;robot does it&#x27;): precision around 85% and near-perfect recall per the text; the naive baseline scores 0 because it always predicts the majority class. Caveats: text says 10 tasks but the Fig. 3 caption says 20 observed goals; Fig. 4 vs 5 referenced inconsistently. Expected to fail in high dimensions; epsilon and kernel width set empirically. Simulation only, no human participants, no published baseline.","Tisnikar, Swieczkowska, Ma, Canal, Leonetti, arXiv 2026 (p28; PDF is v1, July 2026, no venue named). This is the only member that is ad hoc teamwork in the surveys&#x27; sense: Mirsky ST2 (modelling teammates, here capabilities), heterogeneous teammates; Albrecht and Stone type-based reasoning (Bayesian posterior over capability vectors). Edges: CAMO -&gt; CE-CM is kind 0 (the paper says it uses the same approximate Bayesian simulate-and-compare loop; setting and contribution otherwise differ). Adaptive alloc., Task prof. and Robust sched. are kind 2: CE-CM&#x27;s related work says parameter estimation (proficiency, fatigue, capabilities) appears in centralised allocation and scheduling, whose planner controls the team, so they violate the assumption that partners are uncontrollable. Related work also notes preferences are task-specific while capabilities say what is feasible. Ancestry: CE-CM&#x27;s ABC loop comes from CAMO, which reuses Approx. Bayes&#x27;s reach controller and rejection idea; the text we matched does not tie CE-CM to Approx. Bayes directly. Method (Algorithm 1): for each task draw capability guesses (N = 500), plan the joint task under each, accept those whose predicted trajectory is within epsilon of the observed one, pool accepted samples across tasks, estimate each capability as present if its frequency exceeds psi (0.5 in TidyUP), then plan the next task and execute only own actions. CE-CM-Div accepts a guess if any of up to k = 50 diverse rollouts (delta = 0.35) matches. Numbers: human study, Hamming distance after five tasks, Types 1/2/3: CE-CM 0.49/0.75/0.46, CE-CM-Div about 0.29/0.39/0.27 (Section 5.3.2). 15 participants x 15 episodes, 225 trajectories, scored offline. Simulation: TidyUP error near zero within five tasks; Overcooked 0.18-0.20 after five tasks. CE-CM accepts fewer than 10 samples per task on human data, CE-CM-Div 120-175. Caveats: dyads only (joint planning grows exponentially with agents); offline human evaluation, no live trial; in Overcooked corrected actions barely drop. Code link (github.com/Ptisni/CE-CM) not checked.","Numbers (each from its own paper, not comparable): Approx. Bayes task time 36.49 s vs 40.69 s collaborative baseline (Table I, 10 runs per method, UR5e + human, 16 cubes; no participant count or test). Adaptive alloc. all 6 targets in about 1 min (Robotarium, 6 robots, 6 tasks), 3 of 4 robots recovered after a switch, no baselines. Task prof. aggregate makespan gain 44.8 +/- 6.4% (20 tasks) and 18.9 +/- 5.5% (50 tasks) over a fixed EDF schedule in simulation; LEGO study n = 90. Robust sched. 293 vs 203 wins per 500 games against STRATA, 324 vs 151 against random; up to 140 agents and 40 tasks. CAMO about 25 cm vs about 160 cm (bars read by eye: ~22, ~28, ~163). CE-CM human-study Hamming error 0.27-0.39 (Div) vs 0.46-0.75, simulated Overcooked 0.18-0.20 after five tasks. Metrics are different (completion, makespan, wins, cm, Hamming distance); do not rank the papers. Open limits: allocators control all agents; fixed capabilities in CAMO and CE-CM; dyads only in CE-CM; offline human data; exponential scaling of joint planning. Uncertainties: Approx. Bayes Table I has no units (seconds read from Fig. 6) and an undefined +-; venues of p29, p32, p79 from file names; Task prof. p-values inconsistent across the paper; CAMO 10 vs 20 tasks; CE-CM Div values &#x27;approximately&#x27;.","Numbers (each from its own paper, not comparable): Approx. Bayes task time 36.49 s vs 40.69 s collaborative baseline (Table I, 10 runs per method, UR5e + human, 16 cubes; no participant count or test). Adaptive alloc. all 6 targets in about 1 min (Robotarium, 6 robots, 6 tasks), 3 of 4 robots recovered after a switch, no baselines. Task prof. aggregate makespan gain 44.8 +/- 6.4% (20 tasks) and 18.9 +/- 5.5% (50 tasks) over a fixed EDF schedule in simulation; LEGO study n = 90. Robust sched. 293 vs 203 wins per 500 games against STRATA, 324 vs 151 against random; up to 140 agents and 40 tasks. CAMO about 25 cm vs about 160 cm (bars read by eye: ~22, ~28, ~163). CE-CM human-study Hamming error 0.27-0.39 (Div) vs 0.46-0.75, simulated Overcooked 0.18-0.20 after five tasks. Metrics are different (completion, makespan, wins, cm, Hamming distance); do not rank the papers. Open limits: allocators control all agents; fixed capabilities in CAMO and CE-CM; dyads only in CE-CM; offline human data; exponential scaling of joint planning. Uncertainties: Approx. Bayes Table I has no units (seconds read from Fig. 6) and an undefined +-; venues of p29, p32, p79 from file names; Task prof. p-values inconsistent across the paper; CAMO 10 vs 20 tasks; CE-CM Div values &#x27;approximately&#x27;."]});
